\begin_layout Plain Layout
This work aims,
 through image analysis of oil reservoir rocks,
 study,
 simulate and determine the petrophysical properties that describe the multiphase flow in porous medium.
 Such information describes the dynamic interaction between the fluid and the porous media,
 is essential to understanding the behavior of reservoirs producing.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
During Petroleum Engineering training,
 students must develop the ability to interpret subsurface data,
 especially well logs,
 which are widely used in formation evaluation and reservoir characterization.
 However,
 in academic settings,
 access to professional geophysical analysis software—
such as Techlog,
 WellCAD,
 and Petra—
is often limited due to high licensing costs,
 operational complexity,
 and the need for specialized training.
 As a result,
 many students have restricted contact with real logging data,
 which hinders the consolidation of fundamental concepts in petrophysics,
 geology,
 and well engineering.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To address this need,
 this project proposes the development of GeoLogViewer,
 an educational software tool designed for reading,
 interpreting,
 and visualizing LAS (Log ASCII Standard) files.
 The application allows students to manipulate geophysical curves such as Gamma Ray,
 density,
 and porosity in an intuitive and interactive manner,
 facilitating the understanding of lithological variations and formation evaluation processes.
 The system will offer different plotting modes,
 curve comparison features,
 simultaneous visualization of multiple logs,
 interval marking,
 and graph export capabilities,
 making it an essential resource for practical exercises,
 laboratory classes,
 scientific initiation projects,
 and academic activities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GeoLogViewer will be developed in C++ with the Qt framework,
 ensuring high performance in file processing and providing a responsive and user-friendly graphical interface.
 Designed as a free,
 lightweight,
 and modular platform,
 the software aims to democratize access to well log analysis in academic environments,
 bringing students closer to real scenarios encountered in the petroleum industry.
 It is expected that GeoLogViewer will directly contribute to students’ technical development,
 strengthening their ability to interpret geophysical data,
 understand subsurface phenomena,
 and apply theoretical concepts in practical contexts.
